;;; A premise that is not a form is dropped, in silence — **W24**,
;;; register row `W24/bare-premise` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact, `(hit w)`, and three rules with a premise that is not a form:
;;; `a`'s whole `:match` is `_`, `b` writes `_` beside `(hit ?y)`, and `c`'s
;;; whole `:match` is the atom `foo`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A premise is a pattern `(relation argument …)`, a guard, an `(absent
;;; …)`, or an `(and …)` or `(or …)` of premises; `_` and `foo` are none.
;;; Such a premise is refused, as a range and a bare `_` are (Q-M1e2.24,
;;; Q-M1e2.22). Every command that loads the program exits 1 (`ein test`: 2).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The program loaded, and the rule compiler dropped each
;;; such premise (`compile.rs`' `premise`, whose `_ => return Ok(())` arm
;;; took it), so each rule fired as if it were not written: `a` and `c` with
;;; no premise at all, and `b` on `(hit ?y)` alone. Root derived `(r1 z)`,
;;; `(r2 w)` and `(r3 z)`, exit 0, with no word of the premises (`ein saturate --dump
;;; FILE` and `ein solve FILE` at `32736ac8`, release and debug; every mode
;;; and `ein oracle` the same). The engine now refuses the program at the
;;; parse, on every command: at each `_`, which is reserved, and at `foo`, a
;;; name where a premise is written.
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;

(relation hit Node) (relation r1 Node) (relation r2 Node) (relation r3 Node)
(hit w)
(rule a () :match _ :assert (r1 z))
(rule b () :match (and (hit ?y) _) :assert (r2 ?y))
(rule c () :match foo :assert (r3 z))
